      level: 'advanced',
      question: 'What does `type IsString<T> = T extends string ? true : false` return for `string | number`, and why?',
      answer: 'boolean — i.e. true | false. Conditional types distribute over naked type parameters: the union is split, the conditional evaluated per member (string → true, number → false), and the results re-unioned. To treat the union as a whole, wrap both sides in tuples: [T] extends [string] ? true : false → false.',
      deepDive: 'Distribution is the engine of Exclude/Extract/NonNullable. It also explains surprising results like `never` inputs returning never (never is the empty union — zero members, zero results). The tuple trick works because [T] is no longer a naked type parameter.',
      followUps: ['Implement NonNullable using distribution', 'What does IsString<never> evaluate to and why?'],
