      question: 'Give three practical places the never type appears.',
      answer: '(1) Exhaustiveness: assigning the leftover of a switch to never breaks the build when a union grows. (2) Functions that never return — throw helpers, infinite loops — letting control-flow analysis know code after them is unreachable. (3) Type-level filtering: conditional types return never for "no match", and never members vanish from unions, which is how Exclude and key filtering in mapped types work.',
